=== entry a50d8be3e67e
kind: translation
rules: delegate, team-kit, verify-surface, cursor-only
intent: The worker.md line opens the `task` of every `delegate_task` child, since T3 has no separate Project worker, and a `poteto-agent` kickoff adds the lines the Subagents section gives. Skills that load only "from a slash command" become skills that load only when the user starts them, because Codex starts them with `$` (no rule covers it). `/deslop` is optional and loses its plugin name, UI and CLI shipping drives the project's verification skill or T3's preview tools and the shell, and the clause warning off Cursor's built-in babysit skill is dropped. Every other trigger stays verbatim.
--- upstream
- First message → restate the user's goal and the smallest change that meets it, then proceed. Open each subagent brief, PR `## Why`, and final report with the goal.
- Starting any agent, whether a Task subagent or a Project worker → the first line of its kickoff is `Read <path>/worker.md before anything else.`, with `<path>` the absolute path of this skill's folder. The operator's rules live in skills that only load from a slash command, so they reach another agent no other way. Keep the rest of the kickoff to the goal and the task, plus this file's absolute path for a `poteto-agent`.
- A fork you could settle by running something → prototype it per `playbooks/prototype.md` instead of asking.
- Code → name the data shape first. Across a function boundary → **architect**. Fan-out → **swarm**. Bakeoff → **arena**. Contested design → **interrogate**.
- Prose, your reply included → **unslop**. Docs, PR descriptions, commit messages → **technical-writing**.
- Before commit → `/deslop` from the `cursor-team-kit` plugin. Before review → **no-comments**.
- Shipping UI / IDE / CLI → `control-ui` or `control-cli` from `cursor-team-kit`.
- A number you measured → **benchmark-checklist**.
- PR status → the **Babysit** playbook, not Cursor's built-in babysit skill. Landing a green stack → the **Shipping** playbook.
- Bot review comments → judge each on its merits.
- Long or unattended work → **show-me-your-work**.
--- patched
- First message → restate the user's goal and the smallest change that meets it, then proceed. Open each subagent brief, PR `## Why`, and final report with the goal.
- Starting any agent, meaning every `delegate_task` child → the first line of its kickoff (the call's `task`) is `Read <path>/worker.md before anything else.`, with `<path>` the absolute path of this skill's folder. The operator's rules live in skills that only load when the user starts them, so they reach another agent no other way. Keep the rest of the kickoff to the goal and the task, plus the agent-file line and this file's absolute path for a `poteto-agent`, per the Subagents section.
- A fork you could settle by running something → prototype it per `playbooks/prototype.md` instead of asking.
- Code → name the data shape first. Across a function boundary → **architect**. Fan-out → **swarm**. Bakeoff → **arena**. Contested design → **interrogate**.
- Prose, your reply included → **unslop**. Docs, PR descriptions, commit messages → **technical-writing**.
- Before commit → **deslop**, if it is installed. Before review → **no-comments**.
- Shipping UI / IDE / CLI → the project's own verification skill for that surface, if it has one. Otherwise T3's `preview_*` tools for a browser or web UI (`preview_snapshot` with `save: true` for a screenshot the user can see), and the shell for a CLI or TUI.
- A number you measured → **benchmark-checklist**.
- PR status → the **Babysit** playbook. Landing a green stack → the **Shipping** playbook.
- Bot review comments → judge each on its merits.
- Long or unattended work → **show-me-your-work**.

=== entry 87699a4e00ff
kind: translation
rules: agent-brief, invoke
intent: Playbook-step subagents are briefed to read the poteto-agent prompt file (resolved to an absolute path) right after the worker.md line, and are given this skill's path, since T3 has no agent type field. Preserves that the receiving chat runs the playbook itself and that routed skills keep their own briefs. The chat that runs the playbook is named by the skill the user started, with no slash.
--- upstream
**Use `subagent_type: "poteto-agent"` for any subagent you spawn directly inside a playbook step.** The chat that receives `/poteto-mode` runs the playbook itself. Routed workflow skills (`interrogate`, `swarm`) set their own `subagent_type`. Respect it.
--- patched
**Brief any subagent you spawn directly inside a playbook step as a `poteto-agent`.** Resolve `../pstack/agents/poteto-agent.md` (relative to this skill's folder) and this file to absolute paths. After the worker.md line, the brief goes on "Read `<abs>/pstack/agents/poteto-agent.md` and act as that agent. The poteto-mode skill is at `<abs>/poteto-mode/SKILL.md`." The chat where the user starts the **poteto-mode** skill runs the playbook itself. Routed workflow skills (`interrogate`, `swarm`) set their own agent briefs. Respect them.

=== entry 88f38a30b2bb
kind: translation
rules: delegate, background, read-only, capabilities, model-role, invoke, cursor-only
intent: Spawn defaults in T3 terms: async `delegate_task` with the whole brief and its own request ID, no `runtimeMode`, and models from the project models file or willyskills's role defaults, applied as the model-entry snippet says. Keeps upstream's per-role reads, trivial mechanical edits on `swarm workers` included. Drops Project workers and the `generalPurpose` spawn for an inherit-parent line (the snippet omits `target` instead). Keeps the `# defaults: 3` rerun reminder, since the setup-pstack skill writes that line and migrates files without it. The setup-pstack skill is named with no slash where the agent reads it, and as `/setup-pstack` (`$setup-pstack` in Codex) where the user types it.
--- upstream
**Defaults for every `Task` call.** `run_in_background: true`, agent mode (readonly strips MCP), file pointers not inlined context, and an explicit model per role, Project workers included. `/setup-pstack` writes `~/.cursor/rules/pstack-models.mdc`, whose per-role lines override the defaults below and the role models in the routed skills (`arena`, `swarm`, `interrogate`). A role with no line keeps its default. A line of `inherit-parent` or `auto` runs the role on the parent model, so omit `model` and spawn `generalPurpose`, because `poteto-agent` defaults to Opus 5.5. Each code playbook reads its own line (`feature, refactoring`, `bug-fix`, `perf-issue`, or `hillclimb`), prose and judgment read `judgment and prose`, and trivial mechanical edits read `swarm workers`. If the rule has no `# defaults: 3` line, an older setup wrote it. Keep honoring its lines, and tell the user once per session to rerun `/setup-pstack`.
--- patched
**Defaults for every `delegate_task` call.** `mode: "async"`, the inherited runtime mode (never set `runtimeMode`, a narrower mode makes the child wait for approvals no one can give), the whole brief in `task` with file pointers not inlined context, its own `clientRequestId` (reused only to retry that spawn), and an explicit model per role. Call `orchestrator_capabilities` once before the first spawn. Keep each returned `taskId` and `childThreadId`. The child's completion wakes this thread, so end the turn instead of polling, and use `task_status` only for a result needed mid-turn. The **setup-pstack** skill writes {{snippet:models-file}}, whose per-role lines override the defaults below and the role models in the routed skills (`arena`, `swarm`, `interrogate`). A role with no line keeps its default. {{snippet:model-entry}} Each code playbook reads its own line (`feature, refactoring`, `bug-fix`, `perf-issue`, or `hillclimb`), prose and judgment read `judgment and prose`, and trivial mechanical edits read `swarm workers`. If the file has no `# defaults: 3` line, an older setup wrote it. Keep honoring its lines, and tell the user once per session to rerun `/setup-pstack` (`$setup-pstack` in Codex).

=== entry 799788d25bee
kind: translation
rules: model-role
intent: The default list gives each role's willyskills default through model placeholders, one per code role because their defaults differ, with no slug written by hand. Keeps upstream's grouping: code, prose and judgment, trivial edits, and the review and design panels.
--- upstream
- Code, prose, and judgment: `claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-fast`.
- Trivial mechanical edits: `claude-opus-5-5-high-fast`.
- Diverse review and design panels: one runner each on `claude-fable-5-2-high`, `claude-opus-5-5-high-fast`, and `gemini-4-carbon-high`.
--- patched
- Code: `feature, refactoring` on {{model:feature, refactoring}}, `bug-fix` on {{model:bug-fix}}, `perf-issue` on {{model:perf-issue}}, and `hillclimb` on {{model:hillclimb}}.
- Prose and judgment: {{model:judgment and prose}}.
- Trivial mechanical edits: {{model:swarm workers}}.
- Diverse review and design panels: one runner per entry of the `arena runners` line ({{model:arena runners}}) or the `interrogate reviewers` line ({{model:interrogate reviewers}}).

=== entry 523fb84a242d
kind: translation
rules: model-effort, model-role, invoke, same-model-review
intent: The role's effort is a ceiling: keep it for the hardest work, lower it for judgment and routine work, never raise it, and never set fastMode. Preserves that panel seats keep their listed effort. Policy: verification runs on the same model and effort as the work it checks (rule same-model-review), where upstream pins it to the `swarm workers` model. Drops the Opus fast-slug and Gemini top-slug rules and the rejected-slug fallback chain. The setup-pstack skill is named with no slash.
--- upstream
**Match the model to the job.** Start from the role's line or default, keep its family, and set the level the job needs. The `/setup-pstack` budget sets each role's starting level. Panel seats keep their listed level. Raise any other role one step, up to xhigh, for the hardest or subtlest work, lower it one step for judgment, and two or more steps for routine work. No role runs above xhigh. Opus 5.5 always runs its `-fast` slug. Verification always runs on the `swarm workers` model, `claude-opus-5-5-high-fast` by default. A Gemini seat never runs above `gemini-4-carbon-high`, its top slug. If the Task tool rejects a slug, never pass it again. Fall back to the role's line or default, then the calling skill's fallback, then the closest valid slug of the same family, then omit `model`. Say so.
--- patched
**Match the model to the job.** Start from the role's line or default, keep its model, and set the effort the job needs. The **setup-pstack** skill's budget sets each role's starting effort, and that effort is a ceiling. Panel seats keep their listed effort. Keep any other role's effort for the hardest or subtlest work, lower it one step for judgment, and two or more steps for routine work. Never raise it, and never set `fastMode`. Verification runs on the same model and effort as the work it checks, unless the user named another. If a model has no effort option at that level, use its nearest level below. `orchestrator_capabilities` lists each model's options.

=== entry 1ad3e0bea581
kind: translation
rules: todolist
intent: The visible step list is `update_plan` in Codex and a list kept in the reply in Claude Code. Keeps the kebab-case file rule, copying the playbook's steps in first, and the figure-it-out route.
--- upstream
Match the task to a playbook and open its steps at `playbooks/<title>.md`, with the title in kebab case. Copy its steps into the todolist tool first. Large, cross-cutting, or unattended work, or a task no playbook fits, routes to the **figure-it-out** skill.
--- patched
Match the task to a playbook and open its steps at `playbooks/<title>.md`, with the title in kebab case. Copy its steps into your step list first. Codex keeps it with `update_plan`. Claude Code keeps it in the reply and updates it at each phase. Large, cross-cutting, or unattended work, or a task no playbook fits, routes to the **figure-it-out** skill.

=== entry ce7bd647a16c
kind: translation
rules: model-role
intent: Upstream reserves its strongest model (Fable 5.2) for when the user names it or two runs on the default model failed. willyskills's strongest role is `hardest tasks`, so the reservation applies to that role and no model is named by hand. No rule covers a reserved model, so this is the closest faithful version. Keep that difficulty alone never qualifies.
--- upstream
**Fable 5.2 is an exception.** It runs outside a panel only when the user named it for this task or two Opus 5.5 xhigh runs of the same brief failed its check. Say which. Difficulty alone never qualifies.
--- patched
**The `hardest tasks` model is an exception.** A spawn runs on the `hardest tasks` line in {{snippet:models-file}}, else {{model:hardest tasks}}, only when the user named it for this task or two runs of the same brief on the spawn's own role failed its check. Say which. Difficulty alone never qualifies.

=== entry 690c716cedc6
kind: policy
rules: same-model-review
intent: Policy: a second opinion runs on the same model and effort as the work it checks, unless the user names another (rule same-model-review). Upstream now sends it to another vendor's model. Keeps the ownership and own-summary sentences verbatim.
--- upstream
You own every subagent's work. Review the diff and write your own summary. A second opinion is the same prompt on another vendor's model.
--- patched
You own every subagent's work. Review the diff and write your own summary. A second opinion is the same prompt in a fresh subagent on the same model and effort as the work, unless the user named another.
